; A built-in predicate below its arity.
;
; `(eq ?x)` has nothing to compare `?x` with: no reading of it holds or
; fails for a reason the program states.
;
; The grammar refuses it at the parse, at the closing parenthesis, with a
; message and exit 1, as every sibling malformation is refused, rather than
; leaving it to an invariant of the matcher's own: `eq` compares exactly two
; values (Q-M1e.18).
;
; `eq` and `neq` are both shapes of the grammar, so `(eq ?x)` and `(neq ?x)`
; are positioned *parse* errors alike, at the same token, and so are
; `(eq ?x A B)` (`eq_arity_high.ein`) and `(absent)`
; (`absent_arity_zero.ein`).
(relation p T)
(relation q T)

(rule one-armed-guard ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (eq ?x))
  :assert (q ?x))
